## 3.  Parallelism – TPL (Task Parallel Library)

Parallelism is about **running many tasks at the same physical core at the same time**.  
C# gives us two main high‑level APIs:

1. **`Parallel`** – for *loop* or *action* parallelism.
2. **`Parallel LINQ (PLINQ)`** – for *data‑parallel* queries.

### 3.1  Parallel.For / Parallel.ForEach

```csharp
// CPU‑bound work: compute squares of 0..9
Parallel.For(0, 10, i =>
{
    int square = i * i;
    Console.WriteLine($"i={i} square={square} thread={Thread.CurrentThread.ManagedThreadId}");
});
```

### 3.2  PLINQ

```csharp
var numbers = Enumerable.Range(1, 100);
var squares = numbers
    .AsParallel()          // enable parallel query
    .WithDegreeOfParallelism(Environment.ProcessorCount)
    .Select(n => n * n)
    .ToArray();

Console.WriteLine($"First 5 squares: {string.Join(", ", squares.Take(5))}");
```

---

## 4.  A Simple, Complete Example

Let’s combine:

* **I/O‑bound tasks** (`FetchUrlAsync`) – *concurrent*.
* **CPU‑bound parallel work** (`Parallel.For`) – *parallel*.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Linq;
using System.Threading;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

class Program
{
    // 1. Simulate an async I/O operation
    static async Task<string> FetchAsync(string name, int delayMs)
    {
        await Task.Delay(delayMs);
        return $"[Fetched {name} after {delayMs} ms]";
    }

    // 2. CPU‑bound helper
    static void ComputeSquares(int max)
    {
        Parallel.For(0, max + 1, i =>
        {
            int square = i * i;
            // For demo: write only a few lines to avoid flooding the console
            if (i % 20 == 0)
                Console.WriteLine($"square({i}) = {square}  (Thread {Thread.CurrentThread.ManagedThreadId})");
        });
    }

    static async Task Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("=== Parallel & Asynchronous Demo ===");

        /* ------------- 1. Run several async fetches concurrently ------------- */
        var urls = new[] { "https://a.com", "https://b.com", "https://c.com" };
        var fetchTasks = urls.Select((url, idx) => FetchAsync(url, 500 + idx * 300)).ToArray();

        // Start all fetches in parallel
        string[] fetched = await Task.WhenAll(fetchTasks);
        Console.WriteLine("\nAll fetches completed:");
        foreach (var r in fetched) Console.WriteLine(r);

        /* ------------- 2. Run CPU‑bound work in parallel ------------- */
        Console.WriteLine("\nComputing squares in parallel (0‑100):");
        ComputeSquares(100);

        Console.WriteLine("\nDemo finished. Press any key to exit.");
        Console.ReadKey();
    }
}
```

**What you’ll see**

1. **Three fetches** start at roughly the same time; the total elapsed time
   is about the *longest* individual delay (`≈ 1100 ms`), not the sum
   (`≈ 2000 ms`).
2. **Squares** are computed by several worker threads (you’ll see different
   thread IDs). The work is *parallel*—multiple cores are used.

